price on ad \nvalorem basis. \n25. Ruby: Ten per cent. of average sale price on ad \nvalorem basis. \n \n26. Sand for stowing: ** \n \n\n27. Sillimanite: Two point five per cent. of average sale price on \nad valorem  basis. \n28. Silver: \n(i) By-product \n \n \n \n(ii) Primary Silver \n \nSeven per cent. of London Metal Exchange \nPrice chargeable on by -product silver metal \nactually produced. \n \nFive per cent. of London Metal Exchange Silver \nMetal Price chargeable on the contained silver \nmetal in ore produced. \n29. Tin: Seven point five per cent. of London Metal \nExchange Tin metal price chargeable on the \ncontained tin metal in ore produced. \n30. Tungsten: Twenty rupees per unit per cent. of contained \nWO3 per tonne of ore and on pro rata basis. \n31. Uranium: Two per cent. of annual compensation amount \nreceived by M/s. Uranium Corporation of India \nLtd., to be apportioned among the States on the \nbasis of data provided by Department of Atomic \nEnergy. \n32. Vanadium: Twenty per cent. of average sale price on ad \nvalorem basis. \n33. Vermiculite: Five per cent. of average sale price on ad \nvalorem basis. \n34. Wollastonite: Fifteen per cent. of average sale price on ad \nvalorem basis. \n35. Zinc: \n \n(a) Nine point five per cent. of London Metal \nExchange Zinc metal price on ad valorem basis \nchargeable on contained zinc metal in ore \nproduced. \n \n(b) Ten per cent. of London Metal Exchange \nZinc metal price on ad valorem basis chargeable \non contained zinc metal in concentrate \nproduced. \n36. All other minerals not herein \nbefore specified (Kyanite, Perlite, \nRock Salt, Selenite etc.) \n \nTwelve per cent. of average sale price on ad \nvalorem basis. \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_119": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\n \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT\n \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION\n \n \n \nLOK SABHA\n \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.119\n \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 07.12.2015\n \n \n \nEducation in North\n-\nEast\n \n \n \n \n \n*119.         SHRIMA\nTI BIJOYA CHAKRAVART\nY :\n \n \n \nWill the Minister of \nHUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n \n(a) the details of Centrally sponsored schemes in Education sector for the benefit of \nNorth\n-\nEastern States, along with the funds made available and utilised during the last \nthree years; \n \n(b) whether the growt\nh of education is slower in North\n-\nEastern States including Assam \nand the illiterate people are more in comparison to other States like Kerala, Punjab, \nTamil Nadu etc.; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n \n(d) the steps taken by the Government to increase \nliteracy rate in North\n-\nEastern States \nincluding Assam?\n \n \nANSWER\n \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT\n \n \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI)\n \n \nA statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (D) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRE\nD QUESTION NO.119 FOR 7.12.2015 BY SHRIMATI BIJOYA \nCHAKRAVARTY REGARDING \nEDUCATION IN NORTH\n-\nEAST.\n \n \n \n \n(a)\n \n \nThe Centrally Sponsored Schemes, Sarva Shikshya Abhiyan(SSA), Mid\n-\nDay\n-\nMeal Scheme(MDMS), Rashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan(RMSA), Saakshar \nBharat, Rashtriya Uchhatar Shiksha Abhiyan (RUSA) are the major schemes in the \neducation sector being implemented \n \nin the country including North\n-\nEastern States. \nThe year\n-\nwise funds released and utilized in the NER States for the major educational \nschemes in  the last three years is given at Annexure\n-\nI. \n \n(b) & (c)\n \nThe percentage change in the Gross Enrolment Ratio (GE\nR) in 2014\n-\n15 \nover 2012\n-\n13 in Assam in both secondary and higher secondary is higher than the \nStates like Kerala, Punjab and Tamilnadu.  In North East States, Mizoram has the \nhighest literacy rate of 91.33% whereas other 5 States namely Tripura (87.22%), \nS\nikkim (81.42%), Manipur (76.94%), Meghalaya (74.48%), Nagaland (79.55%) have \nliteracy rate above the national average (72.98%) and Assam (72.19%) and Arunachal \nPradesh (65.38%) have literacy rate below the national average.   A statement \nindicating the lit\neracy rate as per Census 2011 and absolute number of illiterates in \nthe North Eastern States and other States/UTs of India is given at Annexure\n-\nII.\n \n(d)\n \nThe Government of India has launched Saakashar Bharat Programme in 2009 \ncovering rural areas of 410 districts in 26 States and one Union Territory of India \nincluding North Eastern States,  that had adult female literacy of 50% or below as per \nCensus 2001. \n38 districts in NER States excluding Mizoram have been covered under \nthe programme. To expedite the implementation of Centrally Sponsored Schemes, the \nshare between Centre and NER States is in the ratio of 90:10 as compared to other \nstates in the country, \nwhere sharing ratio is 60:40. Besides these, the Central \nGovernment is also providing 90% funds under Sarva Shiksha Abhiyan, Mid Day Meal \nScheme, RMSA, etc. for improvement in educational in the NER States. These \nschemes are being implemented in partnershi\np with State Governments to cover the \nentire country, including NER States. The programme seeks to open new schools is \nthose habitations which do not  have schooling facilities and strengthen existing school \ninfrastructure thorough provision of additional \nclass rooms, toilets, drinking water, \nmaintenance grant and school improvement grants. Existing schools with inadequate \n\nteacher strength are provided with additional teachers, while the capacity of existing \nteachers is being strengthened by extensive train\ning, grants for developing teaching\n-\nlearning materials and strengthening of the academic support structure at a cluster, \nblock and district levels, besides hot cooked meals to children in the Government and \nGovernment aided elementary schools. \n \n \n \n*****\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE\n-\nI\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.119 for 7.12.2015 BY SHRIMATI BIJOYA \nCHAKRAVARTY REGARDING \nEDUCATION IN NORTH\n-\nEAST.\n \n \nYear\n-\nwise funds released and expenditure released for the major education\nal CSS in \nNER.\n \n(Rs. In lakhs)\n \nSl.\n \nNo\n \nName of the Scheme\n \n2012\n-\n13\n \n \n \n \n2013\n-\n14\n \n \n2014\n-\n15\n \n \n \n \n \nReleases \n \nExpenditure\n \nReleases \n \nExpenditure\n \nReleases \n \nExpenditure\n \n1\n \nSarva Shiksha \nAbhiyan\n \n251933.00\n \n289025.00\n \n239265.00\n \n247574.00\n \n232896.00\n \n295277.00\n \n2\n \nMid\n-\nDay\n-\nMeal \nScheme\n \n68742.21\n \n78913.87\n \n67570.89\n \n70356.25\n \n73009.80\n \n76021.54\n \n3\n \nRashtriya \nMadhyamik Shiksha \nAbhiyan\n \n44214.17\n \n17333.69\n \n32596.21\n \n25308.68\n \n26633.67\n \n36704.5\n \n4\n \nSaakshar Bharat\n \n450.90\n \n3510.00\n \n2517.40\n \n4447.77\n \n1465.80\n \n2947.33\n \n5\n \nCSS of Teacher \nEducation\n \n8257.28\n \n7838.52\n \n8016.70\n \n6924.59\n \n10265.16\n \n9244.12\n \n6\n \nRashtriya Uchhatar \nShiksha Abhiyan \n \n-\n \n-\n \n6000.00\n \n3829.20\n \n9747.00\n \n9700.00\n \n7\n \nTechnical Education\n-\nTEQIP\n \n1702.00\n \n1702.00\n \n1286.00\n \n1286.00\n \n407.00\n \n407.00\n \n \n\n \nANNEXURE\n-\nII\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (b) &\n \n(c) OF THE LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.119 for 7.12.2015 BY SHRIMATI BIJOYA \nCHAKRAVARTY REGARDING \nEDUCATION IN NORTH\n-\nEAST.\n \n \n \nState\n-\nwise no. of illiterates and literacy rate of age group 7 and above \n \n                                                                                   \n(As per Census 2011)\n \nSl. No.\n \nState/UTs\n \nNo. of illiterate persons\n \nLiteracy Rate\n \nInd