    "ls|LS16_S6_U_193": "Government of India\nMinistry of Finance\nDepartment of Financial Services\nLOK SABHA\nStarred Question No. †*193\nTo be answered on December 11, 2015/Agrahayana 20, 1937(Saka)\nCooperative Banks\nQuestion\n†*193. SHRI SUKHBIR SINGH JAUNAPURIA:\nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of Cooperative Banks registered in the country as on date, State/ UT-wise;\n(b) whether the total assets of the Cooperative Banks and the share thereof in the banking\nsector is not more than five per cent and if so, the details thereof;\n(c) whether most of the Cooperative Banks are facing difficulties and have incurred losses\ndue to short term loan distributed to the farmers and also inclusion of their agricultural\nincome into tax net and if so, the details thereof;\n(d) whether the Government proposes to compensate and provide tax rebate to these\nbanks and if so, the details thereof, State-wise including Rajasthan; and\n(e) the steps taken/being taken by the Government to revive the loss making Cooperative\nBanks?\nAnswer\nThe Minister of Finance\n(Shri Arun Jaitley)\n(a) to (e):- A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n**********\n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a) to (e) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No. *193 to\nbe answered on 11 th December, 2015 regarding Cooperative Banks by Shri Sukhbir Singh\nJaunapuria.\nThere are 1576 Urban Cooperative Banks(UCBs) in the country as on date. The number of Rural\nCooperative Banks is 403, which include 33 State Cooperative Banks(StCBs) and 370 District\nCentral Cooperative Banks(DCCBs). The State-wise list of Urban Cooperative Banks(UCBs), as\nreported by Reserve Bank of India, is given in Annexure-I. As regards the Rural Cooperative\nBanks, the State-wise lists of State Cooperative Banks (StCBs) and District Central Co-operative\nBanks (DCCBs), as reported by National Bank for Agriculture & Rural Development (NABARD),\nare given in Annexure-II & III respectively.\nNABARD has reported that as on 31.3.2015, the total assets of the Indian Banking Sector\n(excluding Urban Cooperative Banks) constituted Rs.13054831.00 crore, out of which the total\nassets of Rural Cooperative Banks, i.e., StCBs and DCCBs account for Rs.607809.00 crore,\nconstituting 4.66%. RBI has reported that the total assets of the UCB sector, as on 31.3.2015,\nwere Rs.4,35,197 crore.\nUnder the Interest Subvention Scheme for Short Term Crop Loans upto Rs.3.00 lakh, the\nCooperative Banks are given a subvention of 2% by the Government in respect of their own\ninvolved funds, for providing crop loans at 7% to farmers. Further, the cooperative banks get short\nterm refinance from NABARD at the interest rate of 4.5% to the extent of 30%-60% (based on\nfactors such as NPA level, location, etc.) of ground level credit flow. Thus, NABARD’s refinance\nallows cooperative banks to earn 0.5% more as compared to the cases when their own funds are\ninvolved.\nSection 80P of the Income-tax Act, 1961 presently provides hundred per cent deduction to co-\noperative societies of profits and gains of business attributable to marketing of agricultural produce\ngrown by its members, purchase of agricultural implements, seeds, livestock or other articles\nintended for agriculture for the purpose of supplying them to its members, processing, without the\naid of power, of the agricultural produce of its members. Besides 100% deduction of profits and\ngains of a primary cooperative society from supply of milk, oilseed, fruits or vegetables raised or\ngrown by its members to federal cooperative society engaged in same business is also available\nunder section 80P of the Income tax Act. At present, there is no proposal to provide any further\nexemption.\n\nIn order to infuse capital in the Cooperative Banks, State Governments can avail of long term loans\nfrom NABARD up to a cap of 25% towards Share Capital contribution to Cooperative Banks.\nSome of the other important measures by RBI/NABARD for improving the financial viability of\nCooperative Banks are as under:\n(i) The Cooperatives have been advised to raise capital by increasing their membership, to\nissue Long term (Subordinated) Debts (LTDs) and Innovative Perpetual Debt Instruments\n(IPDIs) permitted by RBI.\n(ii) Fit & Proper criteria has been prescribed by RBI for appointment of Chief Executive\nOfficers (CEOs) in Cooperative Banks. All States have been advised to amend their Acts\nas per the Constitutional (97 th) Amendment Act, 2011 to appoint minimum two professional\ndirectors on the boards of cooperative banks.\n(iii) Continuous efforts by RBI/ NABARD and other stakeholders have resulted in Cooperative\nBanks implementing Core Banking Solution (CBS).\n(iv) Training of the staff in the new products being offered through CBS by making use of the\ntraining facilities of the Cooperative Training Institutes (CTIs), which are financially\nsupported by NABARD.\n****\n\nAnnexure I referred to in reply to SQ 193 for 11.12.2015\nAnnex - I\nState and Union Territory-wise distribution of UCBs Position as on dated (04.12.2015)\nSr. No. Name State / UT No. of UCBs\n1. Haryana State 7\n2. Himachal Pradesh State 5\n3. Jammu and Kashmir State 4\n4. Punjab State 4\n5. Rajasthan State 37\n6. Delhi State 15\n7. Assam State 8\n8. Manipur State 3\n9. Meghalaya State 3\n10. Mizoram State 1\n11. Tripura State 1\n12. Bihar State 3\n13. Jharkhand State 2\n14. Odisha State 9\n15. Sikkim State 1\n16. West Bengal State 43\n17. Chhattisgarh State 12\n18. Madhya Pradesh State 51\n19. Uttar Pradesh State 67\n20 Uttarakhand State 5\n21. Goa State 6\n22. Gujarat State 225\n23. Maharashtra State 508\n24. Andhra Pradesh State 48\n25. Karnataka State 265\n26. Kerala State 60\n27. Tamil Nadu State 129\n28. Telangana State 53\n29. Puducherry UT 1\nAll India 1,576\n\nAnnexure II referred to in reply to SQ No. 193 for 11.12.2015\nNames of State Cooperative Bank (State-wise)\nSr. No. Name of the Bank\n1 Andaman & Nicobar\n2 Andhra Pradesh\n3 Arunachal Pradesh\n4 Assam\n5 Bihar\n6 Chandigarh\n7 Chhattisgarh\n8 Delhi\n9 Goa\n10 Gujarat\n11 Haryana\n12 Himachal Pradesh\n13 Jammu and Kashmir\n14 Jharkhand\n15 Karnataka\n16 Kerala\n17 Madhya Pradesh\n18 Maharashtra\n19 Manipur\n20 Meghalaya\n21 Mizoram\n22 Nagaland\n23 Orissa\n24 Pondicherry\n25 Punjab\n26 Rajasthan\n27 Sikkim\n28 Tamil Nadu\n29 Telangana\n30 Tripura\n\n31 Uttar Pradesh\n32 Uttarakhand\n33 West Bengal\nSource: NABARD\nAnnexure III referred to in reply to SQ No. 193 for 11.12.2015\nNames Of District Central Cooperative Banks (State-Wise)\nSr.\nNo.\nState Name Of Bank\n1 Telangana Adilabad,Hyderabad,Karimnagar,Khammam,Mahbubnagar,Medak,Nalgonda,Nizamab\nad, Warangal\n2 Andhra\nPradesh\nAnantpur,Chittoor,Eluru,Guntur,Kadapa,Kakinada,Krishna,Kurnool,Nellore,Prakasam,\nSrikakulam,Visakhapatnam,Vizianagaram\n3 Bihar Ara,Aurangabad,Begusarai,Bhagalpur,Gopalganj,Katihar,Khagaria,Magadh,GayaMong\nhyrJamui,Motihari,Muzaffarpur,Nalanda,NationalBettiah,Nawadah,Pataliputra,Purnea,\nRohika,Samastipur,Sasaram-Bhabua,Sitamarhi,Siwan,Vaishali\n4 Chattishgarh Ambikapur, Bilaspur,Durg,Jagdalpur,Raipur,Rajnandgaon\n5 Gujarat Ahmedabad,Amreli,Banaskantha,Baroda,Bharuch,Bhavnagar,Jamnagar,\nJunagadh,Kachchh,Kaira,Kodinartaluka,Mehsana,Panchmahals,Rajkot,\nSabarkantha,Surat,Surendranagar,Valsad\n6 Haryana Ambala,Bhiwani,Faridabad,Fatehabad,Gurgaon,Hissar,Jhajjar,Jind,Kaithal,KarnalKuru\nkshetra,Mehendragarh,Panchakula,Panipat,Rewari,Rohtak,Sirsa,Sonepat,\nYamunanagar\n7 Himachal\nPradesh\nJogindra,Kangra\n8 J&K Anantnag,Baramulla,Jammu\n9 Jharkhand Deogharjamtara,Dhanbad,Dumka,Giridih,Gumla-Simdega,Hazaribagh,Ranchi-\nKhunti,Singhbhum\n10 Karnataka Bangalkot,Banglore,Belgaum,Bellary,Bidar,Bijapur,Chikmagalur,Chitradurga,Dawanger\ne,Gulbarga and Yadgir,Hassan,Kanara,Karnataka (Dharwad),Kodagu,Kolar and\nChickaballapur, Mandya, Mysore Chamarajnagar, Raichur, Shimoga, Southcanara,\nTumkur\n11 Kerala Alappuzha,Ernakulam,Idukki,Kannur,Kasaragod,Kollam,K