    "ls|LS16_S6_U_2": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 2 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER 2015 \n \nLPG NETWORK \n2. DR. BOORA NARSAIAH GOUD:  \nSHRI RADHESHYAM BISWAS:  \nSHRI SIRAJUDDIN AJMAL:  \nSHRI RAM TAHAL CHOUDHARY:  \nSHRI BAIJAYANT JAY PANDA:  \nSHRI G. HARI:  \nDR. MANOJ RAJORIA: \nपेट्रोलियम एवं प्राकृलिक गैस मंत्री \nWill the Minister of PETROLEUM AND NATURAL GAS be pleased to state \n \n(a) whether there is an acute shortage of LPG cylinders in the country including \nNorth Eastern States and if so, the details thereof and the fund sancti oned/spent in \nrural/scheduled areas in the country during the last three years, State/UT-wise;  \n(b) the steps taken by the Government to expand LPG network/agencies/ dealership \nin the country including North Eastern States to balance demand and supply of L PG \ncylinders, State/UT-wise;  \n(c) whether the Government proposes to withdraw LPG subsidy or end up the \nsubsidized LPG facility for selective people in the country and if so, the details \nthereof along with number of persons who left the LPG subsidy and qua ntum of \nsubsidy saved by the Government under 'Give it up' scheme;  \n(d) the details of cases of black marketing/diversion/overcharging/ underweight/ \nirregular home delivery of LPG cylinders reported in the country along with measures \ntaken to check black m arketing/irregularities in distribution of LPG cylinders, \nState/UT/OMC-wise;  \n(e) whether the Government has conducted any flash raids at LPG distribution \nagencies and their godown to curb this malpractice in the country including \nJharkhand and if so, the details thereof along with number of such cases identified \nwhere prior information of raids is leaked by Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) and \nfollow-up action taken by the Government, State/UT/OMC-wise;  \n((f) the details of mechanism/guidelines prescribed in  the Direct Benefit Transfer \nScheme (DBTL)/PAHAL for LPG consumers and number of consumers \nenrolled/benefitted; and  \n(g) the steps taken by the Government to credit LPG subsidy under DBTL/ PAHAL \nfor all genuine recipients in the country along with success made by the Government \nto reduce the subsidy expenditure and quantum of subsidy saved after introduction \nof DBTL? \nANSWER \nपेट्रोलियम एवं प्राकृलिक गैस मंत्रािय में राज्य मंत्री (स्विंत्र प्रभार) (श्री धमेन्द्र प्रधान) \n\nMINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) IN THE MINISTRY OF \nPETROLEUM & NATURAL GAS (SHRI DHARMENDRA PRADHAN) \n(a) Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies(OMCs) have reported that there is no \nscarcity or shortage of LPG cylinders in the country including North Eastern States.  \n \n(b) OMCs aim to increase the National LPG coverage to 75 % with minimum 60 % \ncoverage at State level and at least one distributor in each block by 2019. To \nincrease the penetration in rural and backward areas, Oil Marketing \nCompanies(OMCs) are app ointing new LPG distributors and more than 97 % of  \nblocks are being catered by at least one LPG distributorship. \n(c) At present, there is no such proposal under consideration.  \n However, Government has launched an initiative to encourage domestic LPG \nconsumers, who can afford to pay the market price for LPG, to voluntarily surrender \ntheir LPG subsidy.  As on 26.11.2015, more than 50 lakhs consumers have \nvoluntarily given up/surrendered subsidy on LPG.  Every LPG consumer, who \nsurrenders LPG subsidy, is lin ked to a BPL household who gets LPG connection in \nturn.  \n(d) State/UT-wise details of established cases of irregularities including black \nmarketing/ diversion/ overcharging/ underweight/ irregular home delivery of cylinders \nby LPG distributors, in the last  three years and the current year (till October, 2015) \nare given at Annexure I. \n In all established cases of irregularities, action is taken against the erring \ndistributors as per provisions of prevailing Marketing Discipline Guidelines(MDG) and \ndistributorship agreement. \n \n  OMCs are vigilant to prevent and take action against irregularities against \nLPG distributors. OMCs also carry regular surprise inspections at distributors \npremises, conduct refill audits, surprise checks at customers premises,  en-route \nchecking of delivery vehicles etc.  \n(e) Public Sector Oil Marketing Companies (OMCs) have reported that inspections \nare carried out by their Field Officers, Anti Adulteration Cell and Vigilance \nDepartment at LPG godowns and showrooms of LPG distr ibutorships of the country \nincluding Jharkhand.  \n OMCs have not received any established cases where prior information of \nraids is leaked by their officials.  \n State/UT-wise details (including Jharkhand) of number of inspections carried \nout in the last thr ee years and current year (till October, 2015) are given at \nAnnexure II.  \n(f) & (g) ) LPG consumers who join the PAHAL scheme, get the LPG cylinders at \nmarket price and receive LPG subsidy (as per their entitlement) directly into their \nbank accounts.  To j oin PAHAL, all domestic LPG consumers have been given two \nalternatives in the scheme.  If LPG consumer has Aadhaar number, he/ she requires \nto link it to his/ her LPG consumer ID and bank account.  Alternatively, he/ she can \n\nlink his bank account directly to his/her LPG consumer ID.  As on 26.11.2015,  14.59 \ncrore  have joined the PAHAL Scheme. Subsidy under PAHAL scheme is normally \ntransferred electronically within 48 hours.  \n The amount of LPG subsidy provided during FY 2014-15 was Rs. 40591 crore \nas against the subsidy of Rs. 52231 crore during FY 2013 -14, which amounts to a \nsaving of Rs. 11,640 crore.  This saving in subsidy is mainly due to fall in oil prices \nand implementation of PAHAL Scheme. \n--------- \n  \n\n    \nAnnexure - I \nAnnexure referred to in part (d) of the reply to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 2 for 30.11.2015 regarding \nLPG Network. \nNumber of established cases of irregularities of LPG distributors  of OMCs  \nSTATE/UT 2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 Apr-Oct '15 \nCHANDIGARH 3 11 46 20 \nDELHI 100 60 78 56 \nHARYANA 94 38 93 45 \nHIMACHAL PRADESH 4 2 2 5 \nJAMMU & KASHMIR 16 25 15 84 \nPUNJAB 74 35 82 131 \nRAJASTHAN 55 51 121 118 \nUTTAR PRADESH 758 288 405 207 \nUTTRANCHAL 69 6 26 132 \nSUB TOTAL NORTH 1173 516 868 798 \nANDAMAN & NICOBAR 0 0 0 0 \nARUNACHAL PRADESH 2 0 2 1 \nASSAM 22 1 72 27 \nBIHAR 81 63 173 24 \nJHARKHAND 34 37 23 16 \nMANIPUR 0 1 1 1 \nMEGHALAYA 2 0 10 0 \nMIZORAM 0 4 0 0 \nNAGALAND 0 0 0 0 \nORISSA 47 111 38 105 \nSIKKIM 0 0 0 0 \nTRIPURA 0 2 1 1 \nWEST BENGAL 23 25 32 11 \nSUB TOTAL EAST 211 244 352 186 \nCHATTISGARH 65 17 32 31 \nDADRA & NAGAR HAVELI 0 0 0 0 \nDAMAN & DIU 0 2 1 0 \nGOA 7 9 1 1 \nGUJARAT 110 82 66 109 \nMADHYA PRADESH 104 148 183 138 \nMAHARASHTRA 157 138 218 60 \nSUB TOTAL WEST 443 396 501 339 \nANDHRA PRADESH 114 95 150 69 \nKARNATAKA 154 65 97 78 \nKERALA 47 47 114 10 \n\nLAKSHADWEEP 0 0 0 0 \nPONDICHERRY 2 0 1 1 \nTAMILNADU 61 32 85 84 \nTELANGANA 5 9 55 75 \nSUB TOTAL SOUTH 383 248 502 317 \nALL INDIA 2210 1404 2223 1640 \nAnnexure - II \nAnnexure referred to in part (e) of the reply to Lok Sabha Unstarred Question No. 2 for 30.11.2015 regarding \nLPG Network. \nNumber of inspections carried out of LPG distributors of OMCs \nSTATE/UT 2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 Apr-Oct '15 \nCHANDIGARH 95 101 259 71 \nDELHI 1123 1306 1089 545 \nHARYANA 841 505 952 624 \nHIMACHAL PRADESH 370 756 345 190 \nJAMMU & KASHMIR 477 483 431 258 \nPUNJAB 1160 1207 1344 1172 \nRAJASTHAN 1665 1878 1712 1316 \nUTTAR PRADESH 3511 3643 4921 3452 \nUTTRANCHAL 299 230 423 337 \nSUB TOTAL NORTH 9541 10109 11476 7965 \nANDAMAN & NICOBAR 18 9 29 12 \nARUNACHAL PRADESH 46 30 87 19 \nASSAM 753 1002 664 289 \nBIHAR 1127 983 1317 961 \nJHARKHAND 479 510 626 357 \nMANIPUR 81 104 85 72 \nMEGHALAYA 45 34 52 21 \nMIZORAM 77 89 48 51 \nNAGALAND 94 55 52 19 \nORISSA 564 498 1080 548 \nSIKKIM 33 37 67 66 \nTRIPURA 78 88 48 48 \nWEST BENGAL 1530 1429 1719 1006 \nSUB TOTAL EAST 4925 4868 5874 3469 \nCHATTISGARH 628 571 565 349 \nDADRA & NAGAR HAVELI 11 0 0 0 \nDAMAN & DIU 48 20 0 0 \nGOA 260 177 361 128 \nGUJARAT 2022 2086 1873 1033 \nMADHYA PRADESH 1854 2125 2062 1378 \nMAHARASHTRA 5004 3656 4665 2704 \n\nSUB TOTAL WEST 9827 8635 9526 5592 \nANDHRA PRADESH 3411 3104 2238 1441 \nKARNATAKA 1429 1491 1629 1269 \nKERALA 850 999 1392 948 \nLAKSHADWEEP 0 0 0 0 \nPONDICHERRY 15 31 46 41 \nTAMILNADU 2341 2529 3048 1483 \nTELANGANA 0 0 316 614 \nSUB TOTAL SOUTH 8046 8154 8669 5796 \nALL INDIA 32339 31766 35545 22822 \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_20": "LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*20 \nFOR ANSWER ON 30/11/2015 \n \nMODERNISATION OF STEEL PLANTS \n \n*20. DR. BANSHILAL MAHATO: \nSHRI P. NAGARAJAN: \n \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of various public sector  steel units in the country,  along with the  \ncapacity utilisation thereof; \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes  to expand and modernise all the public  \nsector steel plants; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof along with  the funds allocated for the purpose,  plant-\nwise and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(d) the time by which the expansion  and modernisation projects are likely to be  \ncompleted? \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STEEL AND MINES (SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR) \n \n(a)to(d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the  Lok Sabha. \n \n******** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (D) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED Q UESTION NO.* 20 FOR ANSWER ON 30/1 1/2015 TABLED \nBY SHRI DR. BANSHILAL MAHATO AND SHRI P. NAGARAJAN , MEMBERS OF  \nPARLIAMENT REGARDING MODERNISATION OF STEEL PLANTS. \n \n(a) There are two steel producing Public Sector Undertakings under the \nadministrative control of the Ministry of Steel namely, Steel Authority of India Limited \n(SAIL) and Rashtriya Ispat Nigam Limited (RINL). SAIL has five integrated steel \nplants namely, Bhilai  Steel Plant in Chhattisgarh,  Rourkela Steel Plant in Odhisa, \nIISCO Steel Plant in West Bengal, Durgapur Steel Plant in West Bengal, Bokaro \nSteel Plant in Jharkhand and three special steel plants namely , Alloy Steel P lant in \nWest Bengal, Salem Steel Plant in Tamil Nadu, Visvesvaraya Iron and Steel Plant in \nKarnataka. RINL has one integr ated steel plant namely, Vizag Steel P lant in Andhra \nPradesh. During the financial year 2014 -15, the capacity utilization of SAIL was 79% \nand that of RINL was 113%. \n \n(b)to(d): Steel is a deregulated sector and the role of Government is limited to that of \na facilitator.  The decisions to modernis e and expand steel plants are essentially \ntaken by the respective companies, based on commercial conside rations. SAIL and \nRINL have undertaken modernization and expansion programme funded by their own \nsources. Modernization and expansion  of Rourkela, IISCO,  Durgapur, Bokaro & \nSalem Steel P lants of SAIL and Vizag steel plant of RINL have been completed. \nModernization and expansion of Bhilai Steel Plant is likely to be completed by March, \n2016. \n \n******** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_200": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK  SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No. †200 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2015 \n \nNational Tribal University \n†200. SHRI ABHISHEK SINGH: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a)  the guidelines fixed by the Government to set up National Tribal University at a place;  \n(b)  whether there is any provision of relaxation in these norms in any specific circumstanc es \nand if so, the details thereof;  \n(c)  whether the Government has received any proposal from any State Government to set up \na National Tribal University and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(d)  whether the Government proposes to reconsider the proposals received from Chhattisgarh \nGovernment and if not, the reasons therefor? \n  \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) & (b) There are no specific guidelines to set up National Tribal University . However, \nthe Central Tribal Universities are established with a view t o provide avenues of higher \neducation and research facilities primarily for the tribal population.  \n \n(c) & (d) There is no such proposal u nder consideration of this Ministry. T here is already a \nCentral University in Chhattisgarh to fulfil the aspirations of higher education of tribal youth of \nthe Chhattisgarh.  \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_201": "GOVERNMENT\tOF\tINDIA\t\nMINISTRY\tOF\tHUMAN\tRESOURCE\tDEVELOPMENT\t\nDEPARTMENT\tOF\tHIGHER\tEDUCATION\t\n\t\nLOK\tSABHA\t\nSTARRED\tQUESTION\tNO.\t201\t\nTO\tBE\tANSWERED\t\tON\t14.12.2015\t\nCurriculum\tof\tEducational\tInstitutions\t\n\t\n*201.\tSHRI\tDUSHYANT\tCHAUTALA:\t\n\t\nWill\tthe\tMinister\tof\tHUMAN\tRESOURCE\tDEVELOPMENT\t\tbe\tpleased\tto\tstate:\t\n\t\n(a)\t whether\tthe\tranking\tof\tIndian\tUniversities\tglobally\tis\tabys mally\tlow\tand\tif\tso,\tthe\t\ndetails\tthereof\tand\tthe\treasons\ttherefor;\t\n(b)\t whether\tmost\tof\tthe\tIndian\tU niversities\tand\tHigher\tEducational\tInstitutions\thave\t\noutdated\tcurriculum;\t\n\t(c)\t if\tso,\tthe\treaction\tof\tthe\tGovernment\tthereto;\tand\t\n\t(d)\t the\tsteps\ttaken\tby\tthe\tGover nment\tto\trevamp\tthe\texisting\tcurriculum\tof\tthe\t\nUniversities\tand\tHigher\tEducational\tInstitutions\tand\timprove\tthe\tstandards\tof\t\neducation\tand\tbring\tthem\tat\tpar\twith\tthe\tinternational\tstandards?\t\n\t\nANSWER\t\nMINISTER\tOF\tHUMAN\tRESOURCE\tDEVELOPMENT\t\n(SMT.\tSMRITI\tZUBIN\tIRANI)\t\n\t\n(a) to\t(d):\t\tA\tstatement\tis\tlaid\ton\tthe\ttable\tof\tthe\tHouse.\t\t\n\t\n\t\n*****\t\n\t\t\n\n1st\tPOSITION\t\t\n\t\nSTATEMENT\t REFERRED\t TO\t IN\t REPLY\t TO\t PART\t (a)\t TO\t (d)\t OF\t THE\t LOK\t SABHA\t\nSTARRED\t QUESTION\t NO.\t 201\t FOR\t 14.12.2015\t ASKED\t BY\t SHRI\t DUSHYANT\t\nCHAUTALA,\t HON’BLE\t MEMBER\t OF\t PARLIAMENT\t REGARDING\t Curriculum\t of\t\nEducational\tInstitutions\t\n\t\n(a)to\t(d)\t\t\t\t:\t Several\tpremier\tedu cational\tinstitutions\tin\tIndia\thave\tset\tglobal\tstandards\tin\t\neducation\t and\t research.\t They\t have\t been\t producing\t a\t very\t high\t quality\t human\t resource\t\nwhich\twas\tinstrumental\tin\tbuilding\tnot\tonly\tIndian\teconomy\tbut\t many\tforeign\teconomies.\t\nAlthough\tentry\tof\tIndian\tinstitutions\tof\thigher\teducation\tin\tgl obal\tranking\tsurveys\tlike\tthe\t\nQS\tand\tthe\tTHE\tportrays\ta\tpositive\tand\toptimistic\ttrend\t(detail s\tin\tAnnexure‐I),\tthe\tIndian\t\ninstitutions\tare\tnot\tgetting\ttheir\tdue\tplace\tin\tthe\tglobal\trankings.\t\t\t\nThis\tis\tprimarily\tbecause\tof\tthe\tcriteria\tused\tby\tthese\tAgencies\tfor\tranking,\twhich\tdepend\ta\t\nlot\ton\tthe\tperception\tof\ta\tselect\tgroup\tof\tpersons.\t\t\tWhile\twor king\ton\ttheir\tstrengths,\tthe\t\nIndian\t higher\t education\t institutions\t would\t need\t to\t work\t on\t improving\t their\t perception\t\nindex\tif\tthey\thave\tto\tbetter\ttheir\tglobal\trankings.\t\t\t\nSeveral\t initiatives\t have\t been\t launched\t by\t the\t Government\t for\t im proving\t the\t\nteaching/learning\t process\t and\t the\t research.\t These\t include\t Global\t Initiative\t for\t Academic\t\nNetworks\t(GIAN)\tunder\twhich\tacademicians\tfrom\tforeign\tuniversity\tcome\tto\tteach\tin\tIndia,\t\t\npreparing\t e‐content\t covering\t all\tthe\t subjects\t to\t be\t delivered\t us i n g \tt h e \tS W A Y A M \tp o r t a l , \t\ncreating\t the\t National\t Digital\t Library,\t the\t Choice‐based\t Credit\tSystem\t (CBCS),\t improving\t\ncurricula\tfor\tundergraduate\tcourses,\t\tsystem\tfor\tranking\tthe\tinstitutions\tbased\ton\tobjective\t\ncriteria\tunder\tthe\tNational\tInstitutional\tRanking\tFramework\t(NIRF)\tetc.\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\n\t\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n1st\tPOSITION\t\t\n\t\nANNEXURE‐I\t AS\t REFERRED\t TO\t IN\t REPLY\t TO\t PART\t (a)\t to\t (d)\t OF\t THE\t LOK\t SABHA\t\nSTARRED\t QUESTION\t NO.\t 201\t FOR\t 14.12.2015\t ASKED\t BY\t SHRI\t DUSHYANT\t\nCHAUTALA,\t HON’BLE\t MEMBER\t OF\t PARLIAMENT\t REGARDING\t Curriculum\t of\t\nEducational\tInstitutions\t\n\t\nInstitute\t QS\tRanking\t\n2013\t 2014\t 2015\t\nIIT\tDelhi\t 222 235 179\nIIT\tBombay\t 233 222 202\nIIT\tKanpur\t 295 300 271\nIIT\tKharagpur\t 346 324 286\nIIT\tMadras\t 313 322 254\nIIT\tRoorkee\t 401‐410 461‐470 391\nIIT\tGuwahati\t 601‐650 551‐600 451‐460\nIISc\tBangalore\t NA 147\n\t\nInstitute\t Times\tHigher\tEducation\tRanking\t\t\n2013\t 2014\t 2015\t\nIIT\tBombay\t 251‐275 NA 351‐400\nIIT\tRoorkee\t 351‐400 351‐400 351‐400\nIIsc\tBangalore\t NA NA 276‐300\n\t",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_202": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 202 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 14.12.2015 \n \nONLINE SERVICES UNDER EPFO \n \n*202. SHRI SUDHEER GUPTA: \n          KUNWAR HARIBANSH SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of LABOUR AND  EMPLOYMENT be pleased to \nstate: \n(a)the number of employees registered  under the Employees Provident \nFund Organisation (EPFO), State/UT-wise; \n(b)whether the Government has  launched online services for EPF \ncustomers and if so, the details thereof; \n(c)the number of workers  likely to be  benefited from the services, \nState/UT-wise at present; and \n(d)the other measures taken by the  Government to make the \nfunctioning of  EPFO more transparent and convenient for  all \nstakeholders? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n**                                                   ****** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 202 TO BE ANSWERED ON \n14.12.2015 BY SHRI SUDHEER  GUPTA AND KUNWAR HARIBANSH  \nSINGH REGARDING ONLINE SERVICES UNDER EPFO. \n \n \n(a) On an average, 3.61 crore employees have contributed to the \nProvident Fund (PF) during April, 2015 t o November, 2015. The \nState/UT- wise details are at Annex. \n \n(b)The Government has launched the following online services for \nEmployees’ Provident Fund (EPF) stakeholders : \n(i) For Employers:  \n \n1. Electronic Challan-cum-Receipt (ECR). \n2. Online Registration of Establishment (OLRE). \n3. Online filing of returns by Exempted Establishments. \n \n(ii) For employees: \n \n1. Universal Account Number (UAN). \n2. E-Passbook. \n3. Online payment through NEFT. \n4. Facility for online transfer claim submission. \n \n(iii) For Pensioners: \n \n1. Disbursement of Pensions through the core banking system. \n \n(c)These services will benefit not only the members who are \ncontributing to the fund but also  the workers who are presently not \ncontributing. Total number of such workers is about 6 crore. \n \n(d)The steps taken by the Government to make the functioning of \nEmployees’ Provident Fund Organisation (EPFO)  more transparent and \nconvenient for all sta keholders is given in reply to Part (b) of the \nQuestion above. \n \n \n \n \n\n \nANNEX \n \nANNEX REFFERED TO IN REPLY TO PART TO (a) OF LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. 202 FOR REPLY  ON 14.12.2015 BY SHRI SUDHEER GUPTA AND \nKUNWAR HARIBANSH SINGH REGARDING ONLINE SERVICES UNDER EPFO. \n \n \n******** \nThe State /UT-wise details of Average of Contributing Members (April -November, \n2015). \n \nSl. No. STATE CONTRIBUTING MEMBERS \n1 ANDAMAN AND NICOBAR ISLANDS 10,606 \n2 ANDHRA PRADESH 8,44,228 \n3 ASSAM 2,12,859 \n4 BIHAR 2,73,154 \n5 CHANDIGARH 3,50,431 \n6 CHHATTISGARH 3,41,078 \n7 DELHI 23,91,762 \n8 GOA 1,56,543 \n9 GUJARAT 24,12,906 \n10 HARYANA 18,42,453 \n11 HIMACHAL PRADESH 2,51,103 \n12 JHARKHAND 3,99,465 \n13 KARNATAKA 44,10,138 \n14 KERALA 9,63,975 \n15 MADHYA PRADESH 8,55,375 \n16 MAHARASHTRA 72,11,059 \n17 MEGHALAYA 28,637 \n18 ODISHA 6,28,955 \n19 PUDUCHERRY 92,747 \n20 PUNJAB 6,02,897 \n21 RAJASTHAN 8,44,636 \n22 SIKKIM 1,56,589 \n23 TAMIL NADU 42,81,105 \n24 TELANGANA 23,05,861 \n25 TRIPURA 28,388 \n26 UTTAR PRADESH 15,75,530 \n27 UTTARAKHAND 4,50,072 \n28 WEST BENGAL 22,08,285 \nGrand Total 3,61,30,837 ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_203": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *203  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 14.12.2015 \n \nResearch Project by ICHR \n \n*203. SHRI C. MAHENDRAN:  \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a) the details of research funding schemes, along with the financial aid/grants \nprovided thereunder by the Indian Council of Historical Research (ICHR) during the \nlast three years and the current year;  \n(b) the major research projects undertaken by the Institute in the recent past;  \n(c) whether ICHR has decided to initiate a new research project on the contribution \nof Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose and Indian National Army to the freedom struggle \nand if so, the details thereof; and  \n(d) the funds allocated by the Government for the proposed research project along \nwith likely time by which the project is to be completed?\n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (d):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n**** \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF THE LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *203 FOR 14.12.2015, ASKED BY SHRI C. \nMAHENDRAN, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT, REGARDING RESEARCH \nPROJECT BY ICHR. \n(a): The details of the research funding schemes of ICHR are given at Annexure \nI. A statement of fellowships and grants provided by the ICHR during the last three \nyears including the current year may please be seen at Annexure II. \n(b): The major research projects undertaken by the ICHR in the recent past are as \nfollows: \nSl. \nNo. \nTitle Year of \nCommencement\n1. Dictionary of Martyrs: India's Freedom Struggle \n(1857-1947) \n \n2009 \n2. Documents on Economic History During the \nBritish Rule in Northern and Western India in the \nlate Nineteenth Century: Quality of Life \n \n2012 \n3. Translation of Dutch Sources, 1617-30 \n \n2012 \n4. Survey, Collection and Documentation of Archival \nSources of North East India \n \n2012 \n \n(c) & (d): No Madam. ICHR has not yet decided to initiate any new research \nproject on the contribution of Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose and Indian National \nArmy to the freedom struggle. \n  \n\nANNEXURE I \n \nANNEXURE I IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *203 FOR 14.12.2015,  ASKED BY SHRI C. MAHENDRAN, \nHON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT, REGARDING RESEARCH PROJECT BY \nICHR.. \n \nResearch Funding Schemes of ICHR \n \n\t\n\t\nFellowship / \nGrants \nEligibility Criteria Amount  Mode of Selection \n1. Junior \nResearch  \nFellowship \nRegistered in Ph.D or equivalent in an \nIndian University  \nRs 16,000/- p.m + \ncontingency of Rs \n15,000/- per annum  \nAll India \nExamination \n2. General  \n(Post-Doctoral)  \nFellowship \nPh.D or equivalent thereof from Indian \nor Foreign University or has published \nwork of equivalent merit \nRs 28,000/- p.m + \ncontingency of Rs \n20,000/- per annum \nAll India \nExamination \n3. Senior \nAcademic \nFellowship \nSenior Scholar of repute through \npublished research work \nRs 40,000/- p.m + \ncontingency of Rs \n40,000/- per annum \nInterviewed and \npresentations for \nshortlisted \napplicants \n4. National \nFellowship \nIndian Scholar of distinction who \nwishes to undertake further research of \na fundamental or innovative character \nRs 55,000/- p.m + \ncontingency of Rs \n60,000/- per annum \nNomination by \nICHR \n5. Research \nProjects \n(i)The Research Projects Committee \nmay award a grant for a project of \nresearch in History or on a theme \nsubstantively including an aspect or \naspects of History, undertaken within \nIndia, under a scholar as Project \nDirector, upon an application made by \nor on behalf of the said scholar in \naccordance with the proforma given in \nAnnexure V in Research Funding \nRules of ICHR. \n(ii) Bona fide Institutions with a well \nestablished and respected record of \nhistorical research may apply for \nfunding for projects. \n \nMaximum of Rs \n5,00,000/- \nScreening of \napplications by the \nResearch projects \nCommittee on \nrecommendation \nof Experts \n6. Contingency \n (Study-cum-\nTravel) Grant \nScholars engaged in M.Phil/Ph.D/Post-\ndoctoral Work or carrying independent \nresearch in History  \nMaximum of Rs \n40,000/- for M.Phil \nscholars and Rs \n50,000/- for Ph.D \nscholars \nScreening of \napplications by the \nResearch projects \nCommittee on \nrecommendation \nof Experts \n \n \n7. Grants for \nForeign Travel \nGrants for \n1. Participation in a \nTravel and \nMaintenance \nScreening of \napplications by the \n\nand Maintenance \nAbroad and \nVisits of Foreign \nScholars \nconference/workshop \n2. Indian Scholar who wishes to \nvisit abroad for collection of \nsource material. \nGrant Research projects \nCommittee on \nrecommendation \nof Experts \n8. Publication \nSubsidy \nPublication of :  \n \ni. Doctoral Thesis \nii. Monographs and other \nResearch Work \niii. Proceedings of a \nSeminar/Symposium/Confer\nence \niv. Critically Edited or \nTranslated Source Material \nv. Bibliography and \nDocumentation Work \nvi. Periodical Publications \nvii. Any Other Research- \noriented Work \nviii. Translation into an Indian \nLanguage or into English of \nan important work on History \nMonographs- Rs \n30,000/- or 50 per \ncent of the costs of \nproduction \n(whichever is less), \nIn exceptional \ncases a maximum \nsubsidy of Rs \n1,00,000/- may be \nextended. \nThe subsidy \nawarded for \nperiodical \npublication in or for \none year shall not \nexceed Rupees one \nlakh (Rs.1,00000), \nexcept in the case \nof the proceedings \nof the annual \nsessions of the \nprofessional \norganizations of \nhistorians of \nnational/internation\nal repute where the \nceiling shall be \nRupees one lakh \nfifty thousand \n(Rs.1,50,000) \n \nScreening of \napplications by the \nResearch projects \nCommittee on \nrecommendation \nof Experts \n9. Seminar, \nWorkshop or \nConference \nGrant \nAwarded to Academic and Professional \nOrganisations of Historians at the \nNational, Regional, State and Local \nLevels \nMaximum of Rs \n5,00,000/-  \nScreening of \napplications by the \nResearch projects \nCommittee on \nrecommendation \nof Experts \n\t\n  \n\nANNEXURE II \n \nANNEXURE II IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) OF THE LOK SABHA STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *203 FOR 14.12.2015,  ASKED BY SHRI C. MAHENDRAN, \nHON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT, REGARDING RESEARCH PROJECT BY \nICHR. \n \nFellowships and Grants provided by ICHR under different funding schemes for last \nthree years and the current year \n \n \nSl.No\n. \nResearch \nFunding \nSchemes \nNos. of Fellowships Awarded Total Expenditure (Amount in lakh) \n2012\n-13 \n2013-\n14 \n2014-\n15 \n2015-\n16     \ntill \ndate \n2012-13 2013-14 2014-15\n2015-\n16    till \ndate \n1 \nJunior \nResearch \nFellowship \n80 77 76 77 261.83 290.96 320.12 203.69 \n2 Post Doctoral \nFellowship 10 8 9 9 36.67 42.16 71.65 48.25 \n3 \nSenior \nAcademic \nFellowship \n9 7 9 7 43.53 62.02 76.08 70.62 \n4 National \nFellowship 3 3  - 2 - 31.8 15.9 11.85 \n5 Research \nProject 22 27 15 19 12.8 8.66 24.37 14.64 \n6 Study Grant 44 100 71 72 6.74 10.43 17.72 7.86 \n7 Foreign Travel \nGrant 43 67 28 15 26.76 34.26 40.78 7.59 \n8 \nSeminar/Confe\nrence/ \nSymposia \n121 130 59 77 122.73 160.84 115.98 58.2 \n9 Publication \nSubsidy 111 90 51 35 19.61 17.15 17.27 10.47 \nTOTAL 530.67 658.28 699.87 433.17 \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_204": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 204 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2015 \n \nLiteracy in Tribal Areas \n \n†204. SHRI ASHOK MAHADEORAO NETE: \n \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) the details of the total number of illiterate persons particularly in tribal areas of the \ncountry including Gadchiroli-Chimur of Maharashtra, State/UT-wise;  \n(b) the details of the total number of persons made literate through National Literacy Mission \ntill date particularly in the tribal areas including Gadchiroli -Chimur of Maharashtra during \nthe last three years, State/UT-wise; and  \n(c) the details of the amount spent by the Government in this regard during the said period, \nState/UT-wise? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) State/UT-wise total number of illiterate persons in the 7 + age group, as per the Census \n2011, in 109 Scheduled Tribe Special Focused Districts (ST population more than 25  %) and \nGadchiroli & Chandrapur (including Chimur sub-division) districts is at Annexure-I.   \n(b) Details of total number of persons made literate under Saakshar Bharat, the new variant of \nNational Literacy Mission in ST Special Focused Districts including Gadchiroli District \n(Chandrapur is not a Saakshar Bharat District) during last three years & current year is also at \nAnnexure-I.  \n(c) A statement indicating  State/UT-wise release of central share under Saakshar Bharat \nprogramme during the last three years and current year is at Annexure-II. \n*** \n  \n\nAnnexure-I \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) & (b) OF THE LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 204 FOR 30.11.2015 ASKED BY SHRI ASHOK \nMAHADEORAO NETE REGARDING “LITERACY IN TRIBAL AREAS”.    \n \nState/UT-wise total number of illiterate persons in the 7 + age group, as per the Census 2011,  \nand number of persons made literate under Saakshar Bharat programme during the last three \nyears and current year in 109 Scheduled Tribe Special Focused Districts (ST population more \nthan 25 %) and Gadchiroli & Chandrapur (including Chimur sub-division) districts \nSl. \nNo. \n State /UT District \n(ST Population \n25% and above) \nNo. of \nilliterate \npersons \nTotal number of persons made \nliterate under Saakshar Bharat \nProgramme (SBP) \n2012-13 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \n1 Arunachal \nPradesh \nEast Kameng 25,615 \n \n1,563 1,856 1,157 1,166 \n2   East Siang 23,683 1,185 3,173 2,542 847 \n3   Lower Subansiri 18,653 1,233 2,368 1,274 750 \n4   Papum Pare 30,355 Not covered under SBP \n5   Tawang 18,116 76 1,762 2,457 1,498 \n6   Tirap 43,956 319 1,419 1,078 3,207 \n7   Upper Siang 12,137 435 326 223 534 \n8   Upper Subansiri 25,695 0 1,978 2,736 575 \n9   West Siang 32,673 1,339 2,266 2,508 1,051 \n10 Assam North Cachar Hills \n(Dima Hasao) \n40,815 Not covered under SBP \n11   Karbi Anglong 2,47,418 0 0 7,304 786 \n12 Chhattisgarh Bastar 5,45,623 4,901 31,793 12,044 6,528 \n13   Bilaspur 6,59,234 12,707 51,575 29,529 54,677 \n14   Dantewara 2,62,764 4,388 17,902 7,517 4,225 \n15   Jashpur 2,33,988 5,857 31,444 21,000 12,035 \n16   Kanker 1,92,800 14,584 18,273 11,561 1,712 \n17   Korba 2,85,865 16,988 45,982 14,145 7,374 \n18   Kowardha 2,66,455 0 0 0 17683 \n19   Sarguja 7,91,500 0 27,834 54,142 28,663 \n20 Dadra & \nNagar Haveli \nDadra & Nagar \nHaveli \n69,584 15,686 57 0 0 \n21 Gujarat Dahod 7,05,117 5,527 11,025 7,505 5,445 \n22   Dangs 46,580 0 4,715 2,304 1,313 \n23   Narmada 1,41,838 3,730 6,081 1,707 591 \n24   Panchmahals 5,88,660 1,055 10,601 7,864 9,438 \n25   Vadodara 7,72,735 Not covered under SBP \n26   Valsad 3,19,582 Not covered under SBP \n27 Himachal \nPradesh \nKinnaur 15,176 Not covered under SBP \n28   Lahaul & Spiti 6,594 Not covered under SBP \n29 Jammu & \nKashmir \nKargil 34,638 0 0 0 0 \n30   Leh (Laddakh) 27,701 0 0