    "ls|LS16_S6_U_3": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 3 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30TH NOVEMBER, 2015 \n \nGROWTH RATE OF EMPLOYMENT \n \n3.     SHRI NISHIKANT DUBEY: \n \n  Will the Minister of LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has fixed the targets for annual growth rate of \nemployment in the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the actual job opportunities created during the last  two years and the \ncurrent year; \n(d) whether the annual growth of employment does not commensurate with \nthe growth of economy; \n(e) if so, the details thereof; and \n(f) the steps taken by the Government to address this issue?  \n \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) FOR LABOUR AND EMPLOYMENT \n(SHRI BANDARU DATTATREYA) \n \n \n(a) & (b): The 12 th Five Year Plan has fixed target of achieving 1.4 per cent of \nannual growth rate in employment through increasing employment from 46.86 \ncrore in the beginning of the Plan to 50.24 crore at the end of the Plan period.  \n \n \n(c) to (e): As per results of labour  force surveys on employment and \nunemployment conducted during 2004-05 and 2011-12 by National Sample Survey \nOffice, Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation, estimated \nemployment on usual status basis has increased by 2.31 crore from 45.10 cror e \nduring 2004-05 to 47.41 crore during 2011 -12, registering an annual growth rate \nof 0.46 per cent and real Gross Domestic Product (GDP) grew at 8.5 per cent \nannually during the same period.  \n \n\n \n(f): The Government implements various employment generation s chemes like \nMahatma Gandhi National Rural Employment Guarantee Act (MGNREGA), Swarna \nJayanti Shahri Rozgar Yojana (SJSRY) restructured into National Urban \nLivelihoods Mission (NULM) since September, 2013, Deen Dayal Upadhyaya \nGrameen Kaushalya Yojana (DDU -GKY), and Prime Minister's Employment \nGeneration Programme (PMEGP). In addition, the government is promoting labour -\nintensive manufacturing and increasing employment opportunities by promoting \ntourism and agro-based industries.   \n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_30": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION AND  FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 30 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 1ST DECEMBER, 2015 \n \nREMUNERATIVE PRICES OF ONIONS \n \n*30.     SHRI RAJU SHETTY:  \n            SHRI HARISHCHANDRA CHAVAN:  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE   कृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण ½ãâ¨ããè   \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government is aware that the onion growers are not getting \nremunerative prices for their produce and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b)  the corrective steps taken by the Government to ensure adequate support prices to \nthe farmers;  \n \n(c)  whether there is apprehension of an adverse impact on farmers on account of \nimport of onion during the current year; and  \n \n(d)  if so, the details thereof along with the corrective steps taken by the Government in \nthis regard? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण ½ãâ¨ããè  (SHRI RADHA MOHAN SINGH) \n   \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( d) OF L OK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 30  DUE FOR REPLY ON 1ST DECEMBER, 2015. \n \n(a): As per the available information, there is no report of  onion farmers not getting \nremunerative prices, as evident from the details as under. \nCost of Production and Average Monthly Wholesale Prices of Onion in 2015 \nCost of Production \n(Maharashtra) \n  Rs./ Qtl. \nAverage Monthly Wholesale Prices in 2015 \n(Rs./Qtl.) \n \n \nKharif – 691 \nLate Kharif- 611 \nRabi - 688 \nMonth Prices \nMaharashtra \nJanuary 1171 \nFebruary 1319 \nMarch 1117 \nApril 966 \nMay 1145 \nJune 1485 \nJuly 2021 \nAugust 4010 \nSeptember 3820 \nOctober 2671 \nNovember 2110 \nSource:- \nCost of Production – National Horticulture Research & Development Foundation (NHRDF) \nAverage Monthly Wholesale Prices - AGMARKNET \n*Till 24th November 2015.        \n \n(b) to (d): Government has taken several measures to ensure proper remunerative \nprices to the farmers producing onion. In order to check high prices of onion in the \nmarket, as a temporary measure, Government allowed import of only 2000 tonnes of \nonion.     Hence,    no adverse   impact on farmers has been reported in this regard. The  \nContd…2/- \n \n\n \n-2- \nsteps taken by the Government to ensure remunerative prices to onion farmers are as \nunder:  \n Assistance is provided for development of infrastructure for post \nharvest manag ement and marketing such as storages, wholesale \nmarkets, rural primary market,  Kissan Mandies, etc.  \n Facilitating direct marketing, contract farming, setting up of private \nmarkets, farmer consumer markets and e-markets. \n Implementing Market Intervention Sc heme (MIS) for procurement of \nagricultural and horticultural commodities including onion, under which \nlosses, if any, are shared between the Central Government and the \nState Government concerned on 50:50 basis. National Agricultural \nCooperative Marketing F ederation of India Limited (NAFED) is a \nCentral Agency implementing MIS operations of agricultural \ncommodities including onion. \n Providing market information to farmers on prices and arrivals of \nagricultural commodities in regulated markets on AGMARKNET por tal \nto enable the farmers to get better remunerative prices. \n Export of onion is free, subject to a Minimum Export Price (MEP) \nannounced by the Government from time to time. To boost exports, \nrecently a new scheme has been launched namely “Merchandize \nExports from India (MEIS)” under Foreign Trade Policy 2015 -20, \nwherein the exporters/farmers are incentivized for export of their \ngoods/products including onion to specified markets. \n******* \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_31": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 31 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.11.2015 \n \nFake Faculty in Private Technical Colleges \n \n  31.  SHRI GUTHA SUKENDER REDDY: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the facult y system is rampant in the private technical colleges; \n(b) if so, whether the Government is considerin g of makin g it mandator y to \ncollect the PAN and Aadhaar details of the faculty to curtail fake faculty; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; and \n(d) the total number of colleges that have been identified with fake faculty across \nthe country and the action initiated against those colleges by All India Council \nfor Technical Education? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n(a) (b) & (c): In some cases, duplication of name s of faculty in the database of \nsome of the technical institutions have been found. All India Council for \nTechnical Education (AICTE) initiated action for updating the faculty details \nof all the technical institutions alongwith their PAN and AADHAR Card on \nits web portal to avoid duplication of faculty.  About 33.85% of the faculty \nhave submitted their AADHAR Card details. The percentage of faculty with \nPAN Card details uploaded on AICTE website is 73.91%.  Efforts are being \nmade to obtain data of every faculty. \n(d):   State-wise details of colleges that ha ve been identified with duplicate faculty \nacross the country are at Annexure.  In case, an institute fails to adhere to \nnorms laid down for “Faculty Requirements and Cadre Ratio” , an \nappropriate action is initiated as prescribed in the Approval Process \nHandbook, which could include withdrawal of approval.   \n \n\n \nANNEXURE \n \nAnnexure as referred to in reply to part (d) of the Lok Sabha Unstarred Question \nNumber 31 for reply on 30.11.2015 asked by SHRI GUTHA SUKENDER REDDY \nregarding Fake Faculty in Private Technical Colleges. \n \nSl. No.\n State \nNo. of \nAICTE \napproved \ninstitutes \nTotal Number of\nfaculty as per \nonline data \nNo. of \nduplicate \nfaculty  \nPercentage \n(%) of \nduplicate \nfaculty  \n1 Andhra Pradesh 861 74774 1040 1.39 \n2 Telangana 810 77484 950 1.23 \n3 Gujarat 432 28428 742 2.61 \n4 Karnataka 751 46666 363 0.78 \n5 Maharashtra 1554 82136 2452 2.99 \n6 Orrisa 302 21054 809 3.84 \n7 Tamil Nadu 1352 119546 520 0.43 \n8 Uttar Pradesh 1093 65897 2184 3.31 \n Total 7155 515985 9060 1.76 (Avera ge)\n \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_32": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n (DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 32 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01.12.2015 \n \nMedical Assistance and Insurance \n \n*32.  DR. UDIT RAJ: \n \n Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be p leased \nto state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government/Sports Authority of Indi a (SAI) has \nconstituted any monitoring Committee to ensure 'Medica l \nassistance and insurance' under SAI Training Centres (STC) Scheme \nand if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise; \n \n(b)  the total amount spent for the purpose during e ach of the last \nthree years; \n \n(c)  the number of schemes implemented and the total amo unt \nspent for the development of the Lakshmibai National College of \nPhysical Education (LNCPE), Thiruvananthapuram; and \n \n(d)  whether LNCPE has become a part of the academic wing of \nthe SAI and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR YOUTH \nAFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI SARBANANDA SONOWAL) \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.32 TO BE ASWERED ON 01.12 .2015 \nREGARDING MEDICAL ASSISTANCE AND INSURANCE ASKED BY  \nDR. UDIT RAJ. \n \n(a) & (b): No Madam.  However, the Sports Authority of India (SAI) \nRegional Offices monitor the implementation of the medi cal and \ninsurance policy under the SAI Training Centre Sche me. Apart from \nvarious benefits extended to the sports-persons sel ected under \nvarious SAI Sports Promotional Schemes, the trainees under the SAI \nTraining Centre Scheme (STC) are also provided with medical \nassistance and insurance cover during their trainin g. Under the \naccidental insurance, the trainees are insured for Rs 1.00 lakh and a \npremium of Rs.150/- per trainee is spent for this purpose.    \n \n The Region-wise details of expenditure incurred on  medical \nassistance and insurance under STC Scheme during eac h of the last \nthree years are as under:-  \n(Amount in Rupees) \nSN  Region  2012 -13  2013 -14  2014 -15  \n1 LNCPE, Trivandrum  592894  25362 6 154296  \n2 RC, Kolkata 121465 86670 118761 \n3 NIS Patiala  19539  61451  5,808  \n4 RC, Imphal  42393  44989  30310  \n5 RC, Bhopal  541913  321974  322500  \n6 RC, Bangalore  291000  333000  852541  \n7 RC, Gandhinagar  162458  213245  32796  \n8 RC, Chandigarh  969 42  102800  91472  \n9 RC, Sonepat 32623 45985 32467 \n10  RC, Guwahati  64620  38184  95291  \n11  RC, Lucknow  10537  7335  24432  \n (c )  The following schemes are being implemented in the Sta te of \nKerala under SAI Lakshmibai National College of Physical Education \n(LNCPE). \n1.  Special Area Games (SAG)  \n2.  SAI Training Centre (STC)  \n3.  SAG/STC Extension Centre \n4.  Centre of Excellence (CoE) \n\n \n The total amount spent for the development of LNCPE,  \nThiruvananthapuram (institution) under Capital Works  is Rs.52.15 \ncrores.   \n(d)  Yes, Madam.  LNCPE, Thiruvananthapuram has becom e a  part \nof the academic wing of the SAI.  It is affiliated t o the University of \nKerala and it offers the following courses: \n1.  Bachelor of Physical Education  ( BPEd – 04 years) \n2.  Master of Physical Education  (MPEd – 02 years) \n3.  Master of Philosophy  (M Phil – 01 year) \n4.  NIS Diploma in Sports Coaching (Water Sports – 01 year) \n \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_33": "ORIGINAL IN HINDI \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 33 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01ST  DECEMBER, 2015 \n \nCORRUPTION IN PDS \n \n*33.  SHRI PRATAPRAO JADHAV: \nSHRI RAM TAHAL CHOUDHARY: \n \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION  miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h  be pleased to state: \n \n  (a)  whether the Government is aware of alleged corruption in the Public \nDistribution System(PDS) hindering the supply of essential commodities to \nthe poor people, if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; \n \n(b)  the number and nature of cases of alleged cor ruption/irregularities \nreported and the action  taken thereon during each of the last three years, \nState-wise; and \n \n(c)  the details of the action plan formulated to revamp, strengthen and \nstreamline PDS along with the action taken thereon? \n \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM  VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a)  to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n*** \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to ( c) OF THE STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *33 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 01.12.2015 IN THE LOK SABHA \n \n \n(a) & (b): There have been complaints about irregularities in the functioning \nof the Targeted Public Distribution System (TPDS) including \nleakage/diversion of foodgrains, foodgrains not reaching the intended \nbeneficiaries, issuance of r ation cards to ineligible persons, etc. in some \nStates/regions in the country. TPDS is operated under the joint responsibility \nof the Central and the State/Union Territory (UT) Governments wherein the \noperational responsibilities for implementation of TPDS  within the State/UT \nrest with the State/UT Governments concerned. Therefore, as and when \ncomplaints are received by the Government from individuals and \norganizations as well as through press reports, these are referred to the \nState/UT Governments concerned, if required, for inquiry and appropriate \naction. A State/UT -wise statement indicating number of such complaints \nreceived during the last three years and current year is at Annex –I. \n \nThe Public Distribution System (Control) Order, 2001 notified on \n31.08.2001 mandates the State / UT Governments to carry out all required \naction to ensure smooth functioning of TPDS.  Under the said Order, the \nState / UT Governments are responsible for implementing TPDS and are \ncompetent to take action against those indulging in malpractices in TPDS by \ninvoking provisions of clauses 8 & 9 of the said Order. The PDS (C) Order, \n2001 has been superseded by the TPDS (C) Order, 2015 notified on \n20.03.2015 in consonance with NFSA, 2013.  The provisions of the PDS (C) \nOrder, 2001 shall be effective in States/UTs which have not implemented the \nNFSA or are implementing it only in part.  An offence committed in violation \nof the provisions of these Orders is liable for penal action under the Essential \nCommodities Act, 1955. Thus, both the Orders empower State/UT \nGovernments to take punitive action in case of contravention of relevant \nprovisions of these Orders.  State/UT -wise details regarding action taken \nunder clauses 8 & 9 of the PDS (Control) Order, 2001 during the last three \nyears and current year is at Annex -II. \n \n(c):  A Nine Point Action Plan for curbing the leakages/diversion of \nfoodgrains under TPDS was evolved in July, 2006 in consultation with the \nState/UT Governments for implementation. Details of the action taken under \nthe Nine Point Action Plan as reported by the States/UTs upto 30.06.2015 is \nat Annex -III.  \n…2/ - \n \n\n…3… \n \nIn a continuous endeavour to strengthen and streamline TPDS, the \nGovernment has initiated implementation of Component -I of the plan scheme \non ‘End-to-end Computerisation of TPDS Operations’ during the 12th Five Year \nPlan (2012- 17) to ensure transparency and check leakages in the TPDS \nsystem.  Under the scheme, financial assistance is being provided to \nStates/UTs on cost sharing basis. The cost is being shared on 90:10 basis \nwith North East States and on 50:50 basis with other States/UTs.  Under this \nscheme, as per reports received, as on 31.10.2015, ration card data \ndigitization has been done in 29 States/UTs, ration card data is available on \nWeb Portal in 27 States/UTs, Online allocation is being done in 16 States/UTs, \nsupply-chain management in 8 States/UTs, Transparency Portal set up in 27 \nStates/UTs, online Grievance Redressal Mechanism in 26 and toll free \nhelpline number has been set up in 32 States/UTs.    \n \nTo curb leakages further, under the NFS A inter alia, States/UTs have \nbeen asked to either do cash transfer of food subsidy under Direct Benefit \nScheme (DBT) or install electronic Point of Sale Device (ePoS) at the Fair \nPrice Shops (FPSs).  So far, more than 51,000 FPSs are automated across the \ncountry. \n  \n The National Food Security Act, 2013 (NFSA) also encourages \nprogressive reforms in the TPDS. \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE-I \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS  (a) & (b) OF THE STARRED \nQUESTION NO.  *33  DUE FOR ANSWER ON 01.12.2015 IN THE LOK SABHA \nSTATEMENT:   COMPLAINTS ON TPDS RECEIVED  IN THE DEPARTMENT \nFROM INDIVIDUALS, ORGANISATIONS & THROUGH MEDIA REPORTS \nETC FROM 2012 TO 2015 (upto 30.10.2015) \n     \n \nS. \nNo. \nState/UT 2012 2013 2014 2015 \n1 Andhra Pradesh - 3 4 7 \n2 Arunachal Pradesh - - 1 1 \n3 Assam 1 - 5 31 \n4 Bihar 14 32 55 98 \n5 Chhattisgarh 1 3 3 6 \n6 Delhi 22 37 78 99 \n7 Goa - - - - \n8 Gujarat 3 4 16 8 \n9 Haryana 5 11 26 28 \n10 Himachal Pradesh - - - 4 \n11 J&K 3 - - 4 \n12 Jharkhand 4 8 16 28 \n13 Karnataka 2 6 6 13 \n14 Kerala 4 1 1 14 \n15 Madhya Pradesh 6 17 7 17 \n16 Maharashtra 9 20 25 40 \n17 Manipur 1 2 1 6 \n