 311 1807 \n2014 8226 00 261 1906 \n2015 * * * * \n29 West Bengal 2012 15436 452 01 1213 \n2013 17284 302 04 1840 \n2014 22769 75 01 1401 \n2015 7680 52 00 462 \n30 A&N Islands 2012 316 00 00 17 \n2013 126 00 00 00 \n2014 * * * * \n2015 * * * * \n  \n\n \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the \nState/UT \nYear Number of \ninspections \nNumber of \nraids \nconducted \nNumber of \npersons \narrested/ \nprosecuted/ \nconvicted \nNumber of FPS  \nLicenses suspended/ \nCancelled/ \nShow cause notices \nissued/FIR \nlodged \n31 Chandigarh 2012 00 00 00 00 \n2013 00 00 10 00 \n2014 * * * * \n2015 * * * * \n32 D&N Haveli 2012 * * * * \n2013 08 02 00 00 \n2014 06 02 00 00 \n2015 * * * * \n33 Daman & Diu 2012 * * * * \n2013 * * * * \n2014 * * * * \n2015 * * * * \n34 Lakshadweep 2012 00 00 00 00 \n2013 * * * * \n2014 * * * * \n2015 * * * * \n35 Puducherry \n \n2012 385 770 161 00 \n2013 1026 520 49 01 \n2014 2206 670 13 00 \n2015 511 175 00 00 \nTotal 2012 689891 89415 4137 21830 \n2013 714220 61711 3052 15310 \n2014 415258 33158 593 4443 \n2015 34147 614 51 888 \nGrand Total= \n2012+2013+2014+2015 \n1853516 184898 7833 42471 \n \n* Information not provided \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n \n \nANNEXURE-III \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART ( c) OF THE STARRED \nQUESTION NO.  *33  DUE FOR ANSWER ON 01.12.2015 IN THE LOK SABHA \nDetails of the action taken by States/UTs as reported 30.06.2015 under the Nine Point Action Plan.  \n  \n1 States should undertake a campaign \nto review BPL/AAY list to \neliminate ghost ration cards \nAs per reports received from the State & UT \nGovernments by 30 th June, 2015 , implementation \nof the action plan has resulted in elimination of a \ntotal of 494.50  lakh bogus/ ineligible ration cards \nin 30 States.   \n2 Strict action should be taken \nagainst the guilty to ensure leakage \nfree distribution of food grains \n33 States have reported that action is being taken \nagainst the guilty to ensure leakage free \ndistribution of food grains.  \n3 For sake of transparency, \ninvolvement of elected PRI \nmembers in distribution of food \ngrains be ensured. FPS licenses be \ngiven to S HGs, gram panchayats, \ncooperatives etc. \nThere is involvement of PRIs in Vigilance  \nCommittees to monitor FPS in 29 States/UTs.   \n33 States/UTs have reported FPS being run by \nGram Panchayats, SHGs, and C ooperatives etc. \nOut of about 5.47 lakh FPS in operatio n, about \n1.35 lakh FPS are being run by such organisations.  \n4 Display of BPL and AAY lists by \nfair price shops \nBPL lists at FPS are displayed in 32 States/UTs. \n5 Display of fair price shop -wise and \ndistrict-wise allocations of PDS \ncommodities on web-sites for \npublic scrutiny \nAction regarding putting up district -wise and FPS -\nwise allocation of food grains on websites and \nother prominent places has been initiated in 22 \nStates/UTs.  \n6 Door-step delivery of PDS \ncommodities to fair price shops \n \nDoor-step delivery of food grains to FPS by State \nGovernments instead of letting private \ntransporters to tra nsport goods is being done in \n27 States/UTs as on 30.09.2015 . This reduces \nleakages during the transportation of foodgrains \nand ensures viability of FPS owners. \n7 Ensuring timely availability of \nfoodgrains at FPS and distribution \nof foodgrains by FPS \nAction is being taken by 32 States in this regard. \n8 Training of Vigilance Committee \nmembers  \n27 State/UT Governments have taken up training \nprogrammes for FPS level Vigilance Committees. \nFunds are also being provided by Government of \nIndia under a Plan Scheme for training of TPDS \nofficers/ officials. \n\n9 Computerization of TPDS \noperations, use of IT etc. \n \nComputerisation of TPDS has been taken up across \nthe country. For this purpose, action plans have \nbeen finalized by all States/UTs. Government is \nalso providing technical and financial assistance \nunder a Plan Scheme on End- to-end \nComputerisation of TPDS Operations. \n*** ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_34": "ORIGINAL IN HINDI \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 34 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01ST  DECEMBER, 2015 \n \nCASH SUBSIDY \n \n*34.  SHRI RAJESH VERMA: \nSHRI PRALHAD JOSHI: \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION  miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h  be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the Government proposes  to launch a scheme for direct \ntransfer of  subsidy provided under the Public Distribution System into the \nbank accounts  of the beneficiaries, if so, the details thereof  and the \nbenefits likely to accrue therefrom; \n \n(b)  whether any guidelines have been issued to the States in thi s regard \nand if so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether the said subsidy would be indexed against inflation, if so, the \ndetails thereof and if not, the reasons therefor; and \n \n(d)  the relative merits and demerits of the said scheme? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM  VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a)  to (d): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n*** \n  \n\n \n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) to (d) OF THE STARRED \nQUESTION NO. *34 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 01.12.2015 IN THE LOK SABHA \n \n(a) to (d): Direct Benefit Transfer (DBT) is being implemented on pilot \nbasis in UTs of Chandigarh and Puducherry w.e.f. September 2015 as per \nthe ‘Cash Transfer of Food Subsidy Rules, 2015’, which was notified on 21-\n08-2015, under the National Food Security Act, 2013. The Rules stipulate \nthat the DBT scheme shall be implemented in identified areas for which \nthere is a written consent of the State Government for implementation of \nthe Scheme. Section (5) of the Rules stipulate that the amount of f ood \nsubsidy payable to beneficiaries shall be computed by multiplying entitled \nquantity of foodgrains with difference between 1.25 times applicable \nMinimum Support Price (MSP) and the Central Issue Price (CIP) or as may \nbe revised from time to time by the Central Government.  Since the amount \nof cash transfer is linked to MSP, per unit cash transfer will increase with \nthe annual increase in MSP. Under DBT, cash transfers occur in the bank \naccounts of the beneficiaries directly, thereby ruling out possibilit y of \nleakages. It also leads to savings in operational expenditure involved in \nprocurement of foodgrains, its storage and distribution through Fair Price \nShops. However though the DBT gives a choice to beneficiaries to buy \nfoodgrains from anywhere in the open market, it is not possible to \nascertain actual utilization of cash. Besides, State Govt. also need to \nensure round the year availability of foodgrains with easier access to \nbeneficiaries at a reasonable price. \n \n**** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_35": "O.I.H. \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nDEPARTMENT OF AGRICULTURE, COOPERATION  AND FARMERS WELFARE \n \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 35 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 1ST DECEMBER,  2015 \n \nMSP FOR PULSES \n \n*35.      SHRI C.R. CHAUDHARY:  \n \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE  AND FARMERS WELFARE कृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण ½ãâ¨ããè   \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether the farmers are unable to sell their produce including pulses at Minimum \nSupport Price (MSP) and if so, the details thereof along with the reasons therefor;  \n \n(b)  whether the Government proposes to procure Moong lentil from farmers at Minimum \nSupport Price (MSP); and  \n \n(c)  if so, the minimum support price of Moong lentil during each of the last three years \nand the quantum of Moong lentil procured from farmers durin g the said period, agency -\nwise? \n \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF AGRICULTURE AND FARMERS WELFARE \nकृषि एवं ककसान कल्याण ½ãâ¨ããè   (SHRI RADHA MOHAN SINGH) \n   \n(a) to (c): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO ( c) OF L OK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.  35    DUE FOR REPLY ON 1ST DECEMBER, 2015. \n \n(a): The Government procures farmers produce including pulses under Price Support \nScheme (PSS) at Minimum Support Price (MSP) on a specific proposal as per the PSS \nguidelines  from a  State Government. All such proposals received from the State Govt. \nare considered and necessary inst ructions to the central agencies issued for immediate \ncommencement of procurement by the Government. \n \n(b): No proposal from any State Government was received by National Agricultural \nCooperative Marketing Federation of India Ltd. (NAFED) for procurement of  Moong in \nthe last 5 years.  Further, prices of Moong in the producing states have generally \nprevailed above MSP during the last 5 years. Therefore, procurement of Moong under \nPSS was not necessitated. \n(c): The Minimum Support Price (MSP) of Moong was Rs.4 500, Rs.4600 and \nRs.4650 per quintal respectively in 2013 -14, 2014 -15 & 2015 -16.  For 2015 -16, \nGovernment has also declared a bonus of Rs. 200 over & above MSP of Rs. 4650 per \nquintal.  \n \n****** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S6_U_36": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \nDEPARTMENT OF FOOD AND PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 36 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 01ST  DECEMBER, 2015 \n \nDAMAGE TO FOODGRAINS \n \n*36.  SHRI R. GOPALAKRISHNAN: \nSHRIMATI NEELAM SONKER: \n \nWill the Minister of CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD AND PUBLIC \nDISTRIBUTION  miHkksDrk ekeys] [kk| vkSj lkoZtfud forj.k ea=h  be pleased to state: \n \n(a)  whether foodgrain stocks held by the Food Corporation of India (FCI) \nhave been damaged over the years; \n \n(b)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor indicating the \nnumber and capacity of FCI godowns along with the quantum of \nfoodgrains stored and damaged during each of the last three years and \nthe current year, State-wise; and \n \n(c)  the initiatives taken by FCI and Government to hire and construct \nnew godowns and repair/modernise the existing ones with a view to save \nthe foodgrains  from the damage and also for ensuring effective \ndistribution of the foodgrains? \n \nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF CONSUMER AFFAIRS, FOOD & PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION \n(SHRI RAM  VILAS PASWAN) \n \n(a)  to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n*** \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF THE STARRED \nQUESTION NO 36 DUE FOR ANSWER ON 01.12.2015 IN THE LOK SABHA \n \n(a) & (b): Procured foodgrains are stored by Food Corporation of India (FCI) in \na scientific manner in covered godowns and in CAP (Cover & Plinth) storage. \nScientific codes of practices for safe storage of foodgrains are followed during \nstorage. Inspite of all precautions, some quantity of foodgrains may get \ndamaged/become non-issuable during storage due to various reasons such as \nstorage pest’s attack, leakages in godowns, exposure to rains, floods etc. The \ntrend of accrual of damaged/non-issuable foodgrains in FCI is as under:- \n \nYear Accrued Quantity of \nDamaged Foodgrains (in \nlakh tons) \nPercentage of Damaged \nFoodgrains against Off -\ntake Quantity \n2012-13 0.03 0.01 \n2013-14 0.25 0.05 \n2014-15 0.19 0.04 \n2015-16 (as on 01.11.2015) 0.03 0.01 \n  \n State-wise details of the foodgrains accrued as damaged/non- issuable in \nFCI during last three years and current year are at Annex-I.  \n \n  The number and capacity of FCI godowns during the last three \nyears and current year are as under:   \nAs on Storage Capacity in lakh tons \nwith FCI (Owned and hired) \nNumber of Godowns  \n(Owned and hired) \n31.03.2013 377.35 1984 \n31.03.2014 368.90 1973 \n31.03.2015 356.63 1813 \n31.10.2015 366.87 1981 \n \n State-wise details of number of godowns with FCI as on 31.10.2015 are \nat Annex-2. State-wise details of storage capacity alongwith stock held in FCI \ngodowns during the last three years and current year are at Annex-3,4,5&6.  \n \n(c): To augment the covered storage capacity, construction of godowns has \nbeen undertaken in PPP mode in 20 States under the Private Entrepreneurs \nGuarantee (PEG) Scheme through private sector as well as Central \nWarehousing Corporation ( CWC), State Warehousing Corporations ( SWCs). As \non 31.10.2015, a capacity of 130.31 lakh tons has been created under this \nscheme.  \n...2/-  \n\n \n \n...2... \n \n Government is also using Plan funds for constructing godowns by FCI in \nthe North Eastern States and a fe w select States (Odisha, Kerala, Goa and \nHimachal Pradesh).  Total capacity completed till 31.10.2015 is 88060 tons. \n \n The PPP model is also being used for creating modern storage facilities \nin the form of Silos. FCI has identified 6 locations viz Changsar (Assam), \nNarela(Delhi), Sahnewaal(Punjab), Kotkapura(Punjab), Katihar(Bihar) and \nWhitefield(Karnataka) where land is available for construction of Silo for \nwheat storage and bids (RFQ) have been opened for these places on \n02.06.2015. \n \n In addition, FCI undertakes repairs and maintenance of existing godowns \nwhereby all godo